; rfc-9964 - the ML-DSA COSE_Sign1 and JWS, as instantiated by this document
;
; DERIVED - not verbatim. RFC 9964 defines no message structure at all: it
; registers algorithm code points and key parameters and defers the envelope to
; RFC 9052. Every production below was written by this library, from the
; document's section 5 prose and from decoding its three Appendix A COSE
; examples. Do not cite this file as RFC 9964's grammar.
;
; Built from: section 5 (lines 175-238), Appendix A.2 Figures 6-8 (1454-2872).
; The outer shape is RFC 9052 section 4.2's COSE_Sign1 and is reproduced here
; only so that the ML-DSA specialisation has something to attach to.
;
; THE FINDING THIS FILE CARRIES: RFC 9964 registers NO new COSE header
; parameter and NO new JOSE header parameter. Its IANA section (8.1) touches
; four registries - COSE Algorithms, COSE Key Types, COSE Key Type Parameters,
; JOSE/JWK equivalents - and the COSE Header Parameters registry is not among
; them. The only header parameter an ML-DSA signature needs is `alg` (label 1),
; which RFC 9052 already defines.
;
; Prelude names used (RFC 8610 Appendix D): int, bstr, tstr, nil, any.

label = int / tstr
values = any

ml-dsa-44 = -48
ml-dsa-65 = -49
ml-dsa-87 = -50
ml-dsa-alg = ml-dsa-44 / ml-dsa-65 / ml-dsa-87

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; 1. Headers
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;
; `alg` is label 1 (RFC 9052 section 3.1, Table 2) and carries -48 / -49 / -50
; (section 8.1.1). All three Appendix A examples place `alg` and `kid` in the
; PROTECTED bucket and leave the unprotected bucket empty.
;
; [analysis] Putting `alg` in the protected bucket is not required by RFC 9964
; - it says nothing about header placement - but it is what every example does,
; and for AKP it matters more than usual: `alg` is also inside the key's
; thumbprint, so an unprotected `alg` would be a mutable copy of a value the
; key identifier commits to.

ml-dsa-protected-header = {
  1 => ml-dsa-alg,                ; alg,  REQUIRED in practice; the only
                                  ;       algorithm-specific header there is
  ? 4 => bstr,                    ; kid,  32 octets in every example - the
                                  ;       COSE Key Thumbprint of the signer
  * label => values,
}

ml-dsa-unprotected-header = {
  * label => values,              ; empty in all three examples
}

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; 2. COSE_Sign1
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;
; RFC 9964 section 5: "When producing COSE signatures, no encoding is needed;
; see Section 4 of [RFC9052] for more details on how COSE signatures are
; created."

ML-DSA-COSE-Sign1 = [
  protected: bstr,                ; a bstr wrapping ml-dsa-protected-header;
                                  ; 39 octets in every Appendix A example
  unprotected: ml-dsa-unprotected-header,
  payload: bstr / nil,            ; nil for a detached payload
  signature: ml-dsa-signature,
]

; THE TAG. Appendix A.2 prints every COSE_Sign1 in the TAGGED form - the
; sign1_diag values all begin `18([`, and the sign1 hex all begins 0xd2. Tag 18
; is RFC 9052 section 2 Table 1's, an IANA CBOR Tags allocation. This document
; does not discuss the choice; it simply never prints the untagged form.
ML-DSA-COSE-Sign1-Tagged = #6.18(ML-DSA-COSE-Sign1)

ml-dsa-signature = ML-DSA-44-Signature / ML-DSA-65-Signature / ML-DSA-87-Signature
ML-DSA-44-Signature = bstr .size 2420
ML-DSA-65-Signature = bstr .size 3309
ML-DSA-87-Signature = bstr .size 4627

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; 3. Sig_structure - what is actually signed
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;
; RFC 9052 section 4.4. Reproduced because RFC 9964's `raw_to_be_signed` fields
; are instances of it and are the record's most directly testable fixture: all
; three are 85 octets, differing only in the two bytes of `alg` and the 32 of
; `kid`.

ML-DSA-Sig-structure = [
  context: "Signature1",
  body_protected: bstr,           ; the same 39 octets as `protected` above
  external_aad: bstr,             ; empty (0x40) in all three examples
  payload: bstr,                  ; h'68656c6c6f20706f7374207175616e74756d20
                                  ;   7369676e617475726573' = "hello post
                                  ;   quantum signatures", 29 octets
]

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; 4. JWS
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;
; Section 5: "When producing JSON Web Signatures, the signature byte strings
; are base64url encoded and the encoded signature size is larger than described
; in the table above." The three Appendix A.1 `jws` values are JWS Compact
; Serialization with a protected header of exactly {"alg", "kid"}.
;
; CDDL over a JSON text is a rendering, not the hashed or signed artifact. Kept
; here so the two serialisations sit side by side.

ML-DSA-JWS-Protected-Header = {
  "alg" => "ML-DSA-44" / "ML-DSA-65" / "ML-DSA-87",
  ? "kid" => tstr,                ; the RFC 7638 JWK Thumbprint, base64url
  * tstr => values,
}

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; 5. What this grammar deliberately does NOT constrain
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;
; (a) THAT THE SIGNATURE VERIFIES. Length is all a grammar can check. Section 5
;     points at FIPS 204 Algorithm 2 for signing and section 7.2 of FIPS 204
;     for the encoding; neither is expressible here.
;
; (b) ctx. Section 5: "The ctx parameter MUST be the empty string for
;     ML-DSA-44, ML-DSA-65, and ML-DSA-87." `ctx` is a FIPS 204 signing-input
;     parameter with no COSE or JOSE label, so no structure carries it and no
;     schema can check it. This is the one normative MUST in section 5 that is
;     unobservable from the wire bytes.
;
; (c) WHETHER `kid` IS A THUMBPRINT. Every example's `kid` is the key's COSE Key
;     Thumbprint (verified - see ../examples/vectors.yaml), but section 6 only
;     says "See the kid values in the JWK and COSE Key examples in Appendix A
;     for examples of AKP thumbprints." That is an illustration, not a rule, and
;     `? 4 => bstr` is as far as the grammar may go.
;
; (d) TAG PRESENCE. Both ML-DSA-COSE-Sign1 and ML-DSA-COSE-Sign1-Tagged are
;     defined because RFC 9052 permits either and RFC 9964 exhibits only the
;     tagged one. A validator must be told which it is checking.
;
; (e) HashML-DSA. Section 7.2: "As the verify routines are different, future
;     support for HashML-DSA would require the registration of additional
;     algorithms." No code point, so nothing to admit or exclude.
